# Security headers for the whole agnusdei.ai deployment — and agnusdei.io,
# which serves the identical build from the same Worker (site/ at the root,
# the demo under /bede/ — see scripts/build_pages_site.sh). Read
# by Cloudflare's Workers Static Assets the same way it reads a Pages
# project's _headers file:
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# Mirrors, as closely as a static file allows, the header set the actual
# product already enforces at two layers — homeschool-api/core/middleware.py's
# SecurityHeadersMiddleware and homeschool-tutor/nginx.conf — so the public
# marketing/demo surface isn't held to a lower bar than the self-hosted app
# itself. See docs/SECURITY.md.
#
# ── ONE rule, deliberately. Do not re-split this by path. ─────────────────
#
# The first version of this file had a general `/*` block plus a narrower
# `/bede/*` block that "overrode" connect-src, media-src, worker-src and
# Permissions-Policy so the demo could reach its Render backend and use the
# microphone. That design is unsound, and it took the demo down.
#
# Both patterns match a request for `/bede/`, and when more than one CSP is
# delivered the browser enforces EVERY policy — a resource must be allowed
# by ALL of them. The effective policy is therefore the INTERSECTION, not
# the most specific match:
#
#     /*        connect-src 'self'
#     /bede/*   connect-src 'self' https://*.onrender.com
#     effective connect-src 'self'          ← the backend is blocked
#
# So the narrower block could never widen anything; it could only ever be
# ignored or make things stricter. The demo's fetch to
# bede-demo-api.onrender.com was refused in the browser and never sent —
# no request reached the server at all, which is exactly how it presented:
# Render's logs showed only /health probes, and the demo surfaced its
# generic "Could not reach the server" (that message fires on a bare
# TypeError, which is what a CSP-blocked fetch produces).
#
# Whether Cloudflare actually emits both headers or collapses them to the
# most specific one is a platform detail this repo cannot verify from CI
# (the deployment isn't reachable from the sandbox, and the local harness
# that "verified" the old file simply assumed the answer). A single policy
# is correct under BOTH behaviours, so it does not depend on knowing.
#
# The cost is that the static marketing pages carry the demo's slightly
# wider allowances. That cost is close to zero: those pages make no network
# requests, open no sockets, and use no microphone, so the extra permissions
# are unreachable there. Correctness beats a tightening that only applies to
# pages with nothing to tighten.
#
# If a genuinely path-specific policy is ever needed, it cannot come from
# this file — it needs a real Worker script setting headers per-route.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none
  # microphone=(self): the demo's press-and-hold voice input. Granting it
  # here does not enable anything on its own — the browser still requires an
  # explicit user permission prompt, and only /bede/ ever asks.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), microphone=(self)
  # connect-src: 'self' for the pages themselves, plus the demo's backend.
  # That backend's exact subdomain is set per-deployment by the
  # VITE_DEMO_API_BASE build variable (see docs/DEMO_HOSTING.md), not a fixed
  # value this repo can pin — hence the *.onrender.com scope rather than
  # opening connect-src up broadly.
  # media-src blob: — recorded audio is handed around as blob URLs.
  # media-src data: — NOT optional and easy to lose: useTextToSpeech.ts
  # plays a tiny silent WAV as a data: URI on a user gesture to unlock
  # audio playback for the rest of the session. That is the whole iOS
  # audio-unlock mechanism; block it and Bede goes mute on iPad, which is
  # the primary device this product runs on — and it fails silently, with
  # no error a parent could usefully report.
  # frame-src youtube-nocookie: the home page's click-to-play video.
  #
  # Wikipedia/Wikimedia — picture study, and it takes BOTH directives or it
  # still shows nothing. data/visual_aids.json stores a `wiki_title`, never
  # an image URL (deliberately — see that file's own comment: the catalog
  # stays resilient to Wikimedia file/path changes and we host no images).
  # VisualAidCard.tsx therefore does two separate cross-origin things:
  #   1. connect-src https://en.wikipedia.org — the REST summary lookup,
  #      which is a fetch() and is governed by connect-src, not img-src.
  #   2. img-src https://upload.wikimedia.org — the thumbnail that lookup
  #      returns. Wikimedia serves every file from that one host; the
  #      article host never serves the image itself.
  # Allowing only the first leaves a card that resolves and then cannot
  # paint, which looks identical to the failure being fixed. Both, or
  # neither.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://upload.wikimedia.org; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.onrender.com https://en.wikipedia.org; media-src 'self' blob: data:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self';
